# 🚀 Cloud-Native Pet Project — Full DevOps Stack Showcase

> A hands-on demonstration of modern DevOps practices using AWS, Kubernetes, Helm, ArgoCD, GitHub Actions, Docker, and observability tooling — all wired together in a real, deployable setup.

---

## 🧰 Tech Stack

| Category | Tools |
|---|---|
| ☁️ Cloud Infrastructure | AWS — VPC, ECR, RDS, EKS |
| 🐳 Containerization | Docker |
| ⚙️ Orchestration | Kubernetes |
| 📦 Package Management | Helm |
| 🔄 GitOps / CD | ArgoCD |
| 🏗️ CI/CD | GitHub Actions |
| 📊 Observability | Prometheus + Grafana |
| 🏛️ Infrastructure as Code | Terraform |

---

## 🗺️ Architecture Overview

```
GitHub (appgit + helm-iac)
        │
        ▼
GitHub Actions (CI) ──► Docker Build ──► Push to ECR
        │
        ▼
Updates helm-iac repo (CD trigger)
        │
        ▼
ArgoCD watches helm-iac ──► Syncs Helm chart to EKS
        │
        ▼
EKS Cluster (VPC)
  ├── App Pods (Frontend + Backend)
  ├── RDS (PostgreSQL)
  └── Prometheus + Grafana
```

---

## 📁 Repository Structure

This project is split across two repositories:

| Repo | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `appgit` | Application source code + GitHub Actions CI/CD workflows |
| `helm-iac` | Helm charts + Terraform infrastructure + ArgoCD manifests |

---

## 🛠️ Deployment Guide

### Step 1 — Create S3 Bucket for Terraform Backend

```bash
terraform apply -f /helm-iac/Iac/set-up/0-s3-for-backend.tf
```

This provisions an S3 bucket used to store the Terraform remote state.

---

### Step 2 — Provision AWS Infrastructure

```bash
terraform apply -f /helm-iac/Iac/.
```

> ⏳ This step takes approximately **15 minutes**.

After completion, Terraform outputs will provide:
- 🔗 **2 ECR repository URLs** (frontend + backend)
- 🗄️ **RDS endpoint**
- ⌨️ **kubectl configuration command**

---

### Step 3 — Update Helm Values

Open `/helm-iac/helm-chart/values.yml` and replace the RDS endpoint placeholder with the value from the Terraform output:

```yaml
database:
  host: <your-rds-endpoint-here>
```

---

### Step 4 — Set Up GitHub Repositories

1. Create two GitHub repositories — one for `appgit`, one for `helm-iac`
2. Upload the respective folders to each repo
3. Add the following **repository secrets** to the `appgit` repo:

| Secret | Description |
|---|---|
| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` | AWS credentials |
| `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | AWS credentials |
| `ECR_REPO_BACKEND` | ECR URI for the backend image |
| `ECR_REPO_FRONTEND` | ECR URI for the frontend image |
| `INFRA_REPO_TOKEN` | Personal Access Token for `helm-iac` repo *(Settings → Developer Settings → Personal Access Tokens)* |

---

### Step 5 — Update Configuration References

In `helm-iac/argocd/app.yaml`, set your `helm-iac` repository URL:

```yaml
spec:
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/<your-username>/helm-iac
```

In `appgit/.github/workflows/CI-CD.yml`, update the repo references to point to your own repositories.

---

### Step 6 — Connect to the Cluster & Deploy ArgoCD App

Configure `kubectl` using the command from the Terraform output, then apply the ArgoCD manifest:

```bash
kubectl apply -f /helm-iac/argocd/app.yaml
```

✅ ArgoCD will automatically detect the Helm chart in your `helm-iac` repo and sync the full application to the EKS cluster.

---

## 🔄 CI/CD Flow

```
Code push to appgit
        │
        ▼
GitHub Actions triggers:
  1. Build Docker images
  2. Push to ECR
  3. Update image tags in helm-iac repo
        │
        ▼
ArgoCD detects changes in helm-iac
        │
        ▼
ArgoCD syncs new Helm release to EKS
        │
        ▼
Application updated with zero manual steps
```

---

## 📊 Observability

Prometheus and Grafana are deployed into the cluster and configured to scrape metrics from the application pods, giving you real-time dashboards for:

- Pod health and resource usage
- Request rates and latency
- RDS connection metrics

---

## ⚠️ Security Note

> For simplicity, secrets in this project are passed directly as Kubernetes secrets or environment variables.
> In a production environment, **all secrets should be managed via AWS Secrets Manager** and injected at runtime using tools like the [AWS Secrets and Configuration Provider (ASCP)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/integrating_csi_driver.html).

---

## 💡 Purpose

This project demonstrates how to wire together the full modern DevOps toolchain in a single, cohesive setup — from infrastructure provisioning all the way to automated deployments and observability. It's intentionally built to be transparent, reproducible, and easy to walk through.